\caption(The native tree and matching fix parity. Actual Euler positions identify the repeated hub that shortcutting removes.)
\alttext(A four-spoke tree has four gold odd leaves. A separate panel pairs the four leaves into two matching edges. The recorded Euler order 0,4,3,0,2,1,0 shortens to the closed tour 0,4,3,2,1,0.)

> Why the metric construction has a 3/2 bound

Let OPT be the shortest closed Hamiltonian tour cost. Deleting one edge from an
optimal cycle gives a spanning tree, so the minimum spanning tree T satisfies
w(T)≤OPT. The number of odd-degree vertices O in any undirected graph is even,
because the sum of its degrees is twice its edge count.

Shortcut an optimal cycle to visit only O, using the triangle inequality. Its
cost does not increase. Because |O| is even, alternate edges of that cycle form
two perfect matchings on O. The cheaper one costs at most half the cycle.
A minimum perfect matching M therefore satisfies w(M)≤OPT/2.
Adding M to T makes every degree even and preserves connectivity. An Euler
circuit traverses every edge copy of this multigraph once, with cost w(T)+w(M).
Finally, replacing paths through repeated vertices with direct edges cannot
increase cost in a metric. Thus

$$
w(C)\le w(T)+w(M)\le OPT+\tfrac12 OPT=\tfrac32 OPT.
$$
\alttext(The shortcut cycle costs no more than the tree plus odd-vertex matching, which together cost at most one and a half times the optimum tour.)

This proof needs symmetric nonnegative costs, zero diagonal and the triangle
inequality. Off-diagonal zero costs are harmless: pseudometrics are supported,
and if OPT=0 the bound is an inequality rather than a defined cost ratio.
A successful call alone does not establish these assumptions. The separate
is_tsp_metric() checker tests them, with an optional absolute comparison
tolerance. Passing with tolerance does not certify an exact metric or the exact
same bound. Floating-point sums and near-tied matchings also remain numerical
approximations to the exact-arithmetic proof.

> The original square and a Euclidean control

The original distance matrix rounds both diagonals of a unit square to 1.414.
It is still a metric, and its minimum tour costs 4. We preserve that input,
then compare it with native Euclidean distances computed from the four planar
coordinates. The tiny diagonal difference does not affect this tour's cost.
The arrows show the actual chosen closed order. Tied trees now use an explicit
(weight,u,v) order; a previously unspecified tied tour may change without
changing the API or the optimum cost in this control.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/christofides_square.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<double,double>> points{{0,0},{1,0},{1,1},{0,1}};
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> rounded{{0,1,1.414,1},{1,0,1,1.414},{1.414,1,0,1},{1,1.414,1,0}};
  const auto euclidean=dm::algorithms::euclidean_tsp_distances(points);
  std::print("| square costs | diagonal | metric check | closed tour | cost |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(int variant=0;variant<2;++variant) {
    const auto& d=variant?euclidean:rounded;const auto t=dm::algorithms::christofides_trace(d);
    if(t.result.cost!=4)return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {} | {} | {} |\n",variant?"native Euclidean":"original rounded",d[0][2],dm::algorithms::is_tsp_metric(d,1e-12),t.result.tour,t.result.cost);
  }
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::christofides_trace(rounded);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,900);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=1.8;node.height=1.3;node.font_size=20;
  for(std::size_t v=0;v<points.size();++v) {
    node.fill=v?dm::graphics::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{254,240,138};
    diagram.node(std::format("v{}",v),{6*points[v].first,6*points[v].second},std::format("vertex {}\n({},{})",v,points[v].first,points[v].second),node);
  }
  for(std::size_t i=1;i<t.result.tour.size();++i)diagram.edge(std::format("v{}",t.result.tour[i-1]),std::format("v{}",t.result.tour[i]));
  diagram.text({3,8},std::format("Original square: actual closed tour, cost {}",t.result.cost),22)
      .text({3,-2},std::format("Start and finish at gold vertex {}; order {}",t.result.tour.front(),t.result.tour),18)
      .save_svg("build/christofides_square.svg");
  const auto parallel=dm::algorithms::christofides_trace({{0,2},{2,0}});
  if(parallel.euler_edge_ids.size()!=2 || parallel.euler_edge_ids[0]==parallel.euler_edge_ids[1])return 1;
  std::print("\nTwo-vertex control: tree and matching have equal endpoints but distinct edge identities {}; closed cost {}.\n",parallel.euler_edge_ids,parallel.result.cost);
}
