\image(figures/christofides_matching_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual matching work and allocated entries grow exponentially with the odd set. A 64-vertex line still needs only four matching table entries.)
\alttext(The vertical axis is log base two of actual counts. Table storage doubles with each additional odd vertex; partner relaxations grow faster by a factor proportional to k. These are operation and storage counts, not seconds.)

With n total vertices and k odd tree vertices, this implementation materialises
and sorts the complete upper-triangle edge list, then performs subset matching.
Its work is O(n² log(n+1)+k·2^k), and auxiliary storage is O(n²+2^k).
The original Christofides algorithm uses polynomial minimum-weight matching;
this library's bounded subset substitute must not inherit its O(n³) claim.
The default limit is 22 odd vertices, not 22 total vertices. Increasing total
vertices can still exhaust the complete graph's quadratic storage.

> A successful return does not establish a metric bound

Consider a symmetric four-vertex matrix whose off-diagonal costs are all 1
except d(1,3)=d(3,1)=100. It violates the triangle inequality through vertex 0.
The tour 0,1,2,3,0 costs 4, and no four-edge tour can cost less because every
off-diagonal edge costs at least 1. Yet the deterministic construction's
shortcut can replace the cheap path 1,0,3 by the direct edge of cost 100.
The resulting tour costs 103. Its ratio to the proven optimum is 25.75,
which is not constrained by the metric theorem.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> nonmetric{{0,1,1,1},{1,0,1,100},{1,1,0,1},{1,100,1,0}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::christofides_trace(nonmetric);
  if(dm::algorithms::is_tsp_metric(nonmetric) || t.result.cost!=103)return 1;
  std::print("Symmetric nonmetric input: metric check {}, Euler order {} costs {}, shortcut order {} costs {}; ratio to OPT=4 is {}.\n",
      dm::algorithms::is_tsp_metric(nonmetric),t.euler_vertices,t.euler_cost,t.result.tour,t.result.cost,t.result.cost/4);
  const auto asymmetric=dm::algorithms::christofides_trace({{0,1,10},{9,0,2},{3,4,0}});
  std::print("Asymmetric input remains accepted: upper-triangle tree + matching cost {}, actual directed Euler cost {}, final cost {}.\n",
      asymmetric.tree_cost+asymmetric.matching_cost,asymmetric.euler_cost,asymmetric.result.cost);
  const auto largest=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();bool overflow=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::christofides_tsp({{0,largest},{largest,0}});}catch(const std::overflow_error&){overflow=true;}
  if(!overflow)return 1;std::print("Finite entries but an unrepresentable final total reject with overflow_error: {}.\n",overflow);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Symmetric nonmetric input: metric check false, Euler order [0, 1, 0, 3, 2, 0] costs 5, shortcut order [0, 1, 3, 2, 0] costs 103; ratio to OPT=4 is 25.75.
Asymmetric input remains accepted: upper-triangle tree + matching cost 13, actual directed Euler cost 23, final cost 23.
Finite entries but an unrepresentable final total reject with overflow_error: true.
// result_end
\caption(A checked nonmetric counterexample shows shortcutting increasing cost; directed and representational limits are separate.)

The legacy API continues to accept any finite nonnegative square matrix.
For asymmetric inputs, the tree and matching use upper-triangle weights while
the final route cost uses directed entries. Their sum need not equal the Euler
walk cost. No directed-TSP or nonmetric approximation guarantee is claimed.
The new kernel accumulates matching and route costs in long double and checks
the final conversion to double. This also prevents a previously overflowing
matching table sum from leaving an invalid partner index for reconstruction.
Long double remains finite and platform-dependent; this is not arbitrary
precision or exact-real optimisation.

> Caps, validation and evidence

ChristofidesOptions retains the default maximum of 22 odd vertices and allows
a lower hard cap, including zero. Its positive max_matching_states limits
allocated 2^k slots before that allocation; zero disables this table cap.
The tree must already exist to determine k. These controls do not cap matrix,
complete-edge-list or tree storage, nor do they return partial results on failure.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::algorithms::hub_tsp_distances(4);
  dm::algorithms::ChristofidesOptions cap;cap.max_odd_vertices=4;cap.max_matching_states=16;
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::christofides_tsp_with_options(d,0,cap);
  bool table_cap=false;cap.max_matching_states=15;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::christofides_trace(d,0,cap);}catch(const std::length_error&){table_cap=true;}
  bool odd_cap=false;cap.max_matching_states=0;cap.max_odd_vertices=3;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::christofides_trace(d,0,cap);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){odd_cap=true;}
  bool invalid=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::is_tsp_metric({{1,0},{0,std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN()}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){invalid=true;}
  if(exact.cost!=6.5 || !table_cap || !odd_cap || !invalid)return 1;
  std::print("Exact 16-slot cap returns cost {}. Cap 15 rejects: {}. Odd cap three rejects: {}.\n",exact.cost,table_cap,odd_cap);
  std::print("Full validation finds trailing NaN before an earlier diagonal failure: {}.\n",invalid);
  const auto empty=dm::algorithms::christofides_tsp({},999);const auto singleton=dm::algorithms::christofides_tsp({{0}});
  std::print("Empty input: tour {}, cost {}. Singleton: tour {}, cost {}.\n",empty.tour,empty.cost,singleton.tour,singleton.cost);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact 16-slot cap returns cost 6.5. Cap 15 rejects: true. Odd cap three rejects: true.
Full validation finds trailing NaN before an earlier diagonal failure: true.
Empty input: tour [], cost 0. Singleton: tour [0, 0], cost 0.
// result_end
\caption(Exact and one-below table caps, odd-set limits, whole-matrix validation and boundary inputs have explicit behavior.)

Every matrix row and entry validates before construction or an early metric
comparison. Empty input returns no vertices and zero cost, ignoring the start;
a singleton returns its closed two-entry order. Calls share no mutable state,
borrow inputs without mutation, and return owned records. Euler traversal and
union-find compression are iterative. Stage records add linear storage and
retain no DP table after return.

Development tests exhaust all 729 symmetric four-vertex matrices with edge costs
0, 1 or 2 and independently check the MST with Prim's method, the odd matching
with recursive pairing, Euler edge identities, first-visit positions and costs.
For 120 generated integer shortest-path metrics, exhaustive Hamiltonian orders
verify the bound and each component inequality. Another 1,000 planar inputs
exercise generated distances, repeated points, every stage and numerical ties.
Parallel copies, every square start, zero costs, whole-matrix invalid input,
exact/one-below caps, owned copies, wide matching alternatives with fitting final
results, final overflow and a 1,200-vertex line are covered. All fifteen new
cases pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan, alongside existing tour regressions.
No observational dataset is needed for these controlled graph identities.

> What the construction establishes

Parity correction turns a connected tree into an Eulerian multigraph. In a
metric, first-visit shortcutting preserves feasibility without increasing cost,
and the tree/matching bounds give the 3/2 theorem. The native trace now makes
each of those stages inspectable. Nonmetric examples and exponential subset
matching delimit what this particular implementation promises.

> Reference

- Nicos Christofides (1976), *Worst-Case Analysis of a New Heuristic for the
  Travelling Salesman Problem*, Management Sciences Research Report 388,
  Carnegie-Mellon University. https://www.ibr.cs.tu-bs.de/courses/ss21/na/uebungen/ADA025602.pdf
  The library follows its tree/matching/Euler/shortcut construction, substituting
  bounded subset dynamic programming for polynomial minimum-weight matching.
